            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for to go round, then the French for to turn back."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: l'impasse",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "l'impasse — \"the dead end; the deadlock\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "L'hôtel est au fond d'une impasse calme. The hotel is at the end of a quiet dead end."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A feminine noun. Figurative: être dans l'impasse — to be at a deadlock."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "impasse is built from in- 'not' and passe, from passer 'to pass' (EtymDB), which goes back to Vulgar Latin passāre, from passus 'a step'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
